---
import TestPage from '../../../components/us/TestPage.astro';
import TestCite from '../../../components/us/TestCite.astro';
import ThermalCompare from '../../../components/ThermalCompare.astro';
import { S } from '../../../lib/us/tests';

const src = {
  c1060: S('infrared', 'C1060'),
  e1186: S('infrared', 'E1186'),
  doe: S('infrared', 'Professional Home Energy Assessments'),
  hersFind: S('hersIndex', 'Find a HERS Rater'),
  energyStar: S('insulation', 'Recommended Home Insulation R-Values'),
  combustion: S('combustionSafety', 'Combustion Appliance Zone'),
};

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Does an infrared scan prove insulation is missing?',
    a: 'Not on its own. ASTM C1060 treats the scan as a qualitative inspection and says proof of missing insulation needs an independent check, such as a visual inspection of the cavity.',
    src: [src.c1060],
  },
  {
    q: 'Why should the blower door run during the scan?',
    a: 'ASTM E1186 says forced air leakage, pressurizing or depressurizing the building, is needed because leaks are hard to find under normal weather. The blower door does that.',
    src: [src.e1186],
  },
  {
    q: 'Is an infrared scan part of a standard energy audit?',
    a: 'Many are. DOE says many professional home energy assessments include a blower door test and a thermographic scan. Ask before you book.',
    src: [src.doe],
  },
  {
    q: 'What does an infrared scan cost?',
    a: 'No official source publishes a price, so we don’t quote one. DOE says many professional assessments include a thermographic scan, so ask what’s included when you get quotes.',
    src: [src.doe],
  },
];

const sources = [src.c1060, src.e1186, src.doe, src.hersFind, src.energyStar, src.combustion];
---

<TestPage
  slug="thermal-imaging"
  title="Infrared (thermal) home scan: ASTM C1060 and E1186, and how to get one | AuditYourHome"
  description="An infrared scan finds missing insulation and air leaks. What the ASTM standards say, why the house should be under blower-door pressure, and how to get a scan."
  heroTitle="Infrared scan: see where the insulation is missing and the air gets in"
  glance={[
    { k: 'Insulation practice', v: 'ASTM C1060-23', src: src.c1060, srcLabel: 'ASTM' },
    { k: 'Air-leak practice', v: 'ASTM E1186-22', src: src.e1186, srcLabel: 'ASTM' },
    { k: 'Pair it with', v: 'A blower door test', src: src.e1186, srcLabel: 'ASTM E1186' },
    { k: 'Typical cost', v: 'Not published' },
  ]}
  faqs={faqs}
  sources={sources}
  leadHeading="Want an infrared scan with your audit?"
  leadSub="Email us your ZIP code and the kind of home you have. A person reads every message and replies with certified auditors who serve your area."
>
  <Fragment slot="lede">
    An infrared camera shows surface temperatures, so missing insulation and air leaks show up as warm
    or cold patches on walls and ceilings. It surveys a whole house quickly, but under the ASTM
    practices it’s a qualitative tool: it tells you where to look, and other checks confirm what’s
    there.
  </Fragment>

  <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="what-h">
    <h2 id="what-h">What the scan finds</h2>
    <div class="std-grid">
      <div class="std">
        <p class="sv">ASTM C1060-23</p>
        <p class="sl">Insulation in framed walls, ceilings, roofs and floors</p>
        <p>A qualitative infrared inspection for missing, partial, poorly installed or damaged cavity insulation. Proof of missing insulation needs an independent check, such as looking inside.</p>
        <TestCite src={src.c1060} labels={['ASTM C1060-23 (updated Mar 16, 2023)']} />
      </div>
      <div class="std heat">
        <p class="sv">ASTM E1186-22</p>
        <p class="sl">Air leak locations</p>
        <p>Lists ways to find leaks: infrared scanning, smoke tracer, theatrical fog, anemometer, sound, bubble and tracer gas. Infrared surveys large areas fast. The building should be pressurized or depressurized, because leaks are hard to find under normal weather.</p>
        <TestCite src={src.e1186} labels={['ASTM E1186-22 (updated Oct 11, 2022)']} />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="see-h">
    <h2 id="see-h">What it looks like</h2>
    <div class="tc-wrap">
      <ThermalCompare caption="Illustration, not a measurement: the same house in visible light and as an infrared camera might show it on a cold night. Drag to compare." />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec prose" aria-labelledby="get-h">
    <h2 id="get-h">How to get it done</h2>
    <p>
      DOE says many professional home energy assessments pair a blower door test with a thermographic
      scan. Ask for the scan to be done while the blower door holds the house under pressure, as
      ASTM E1186 describes.
    </p>
    <p>
      What it costs: no official source publishes a price, so we don’t quote one. See
      <a href="/us/cost/">what an audit costs</a> for how audit pricing works.
    </p>
    <ul class="linkrow">
      <li><a href="/us/find/">Find a certified auditor</a></li>
      <li><a href={src.hersFind.href} rel="noopener">RESNET: find a HERS rater&nbsp;↗</a></li>
    </ul>
    <TestCite src={[src.doe, src.e1186]} labels={['DOE professional assessments', 'ASTM E1186']} />
  </section>

  <section class="sec prose" aria-labelledby="fix-h">
    <h2 id="fix-h">If the scan finds a problem</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Suspected missing insulation:</strong> confirm it with a look inside the cavity, as ASTM C1060 requires for proof, then plan the fix against ENERGY STAR’s recommended levels in the <a href="/us/tests/attic-inspection/">attic insulation guide</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>Air leaks:</strong> seal them, then retest with the blower door to see what changed.</li>
      <li><strong>Natural-draft gas, oil or propane appliances?</strong> DOE calls for combustion appliance zone testing during air sealing; see the <a href="/us/tests/combustion-safety/">combustion safety guide</a>.</li>
    </ul>
    <TestCite src={[src.c1060, src.energyStar, src.combustion]} labels={['ASTM C1060', 'ENERGY STAR R-values', 'DOE BASC, CAZ testing']} />
  </section>
</TestPage>

<style>
  .tc-wrap { margin: 10px 0 0; padding: 18px; background: var(--navy); border-radius: var(--radius-lg); max-width: 820px; }
  .tc-wrap :global(figcaption) { color: var(--navy-muted); }
</style>
